# vibe-talk launcher configuration — EXAMPLE. Copy it, then fill it in:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/vibe-talk
#   cp vibe-talk/vibe-talk.env.example ~/.config/vibe-talk/env
#   $EDITOR ~/.config/vibe-talk/env
#   vibe-talk/scripts/run.sh
#
# This file is sourced by a shell (vibe-talk/scripts/run.sh), so it is shell syntax: NAME=value,
# no spaces around `=`, and quote anything containing a space, a comma or a colon.
#
# WHERE IT IS READ FROM, in increasing precedence:
#   1. ~/.config/vibe-talk/env      — the one and only default location, shared by every checkout
#                                     on this machine.
#   2. Variables already exported in your shell — these beat the file.
# Passing `--config FILE` to run.sh replaces the file layer: that file, and nothing else.
#
# There is deliberately no second, in-checkout config location. One file that is always the live
# one beats two files and a rule about which wins.
#
# NEVER commit a filled-in copy. Keeping the real file under ~/.config puts it outside every git
# checkout; if you keep yours somewhere else, make sure that path is ignored.
#
# This is the LAUNCHER's environment-variable configuration. It is a different thing from
# vibe-talk.example.toml, which is the SERVER's own config-file format for the same settings —
# the server reads either, and the environment wins. Use this file with scripts/run.sh; use the
# TOML when you would rather mount a config file into the container.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. run.sh refuses to build or launch unless all four are set to non-placeholder values.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Bot token for the SECOND, dedicated Discord bot — not the one your coding agents use.
VIBE_TALK_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=REPLACE-ME

# The credentials THIS server demands of its own callers (not Discord's, not ElevenLabs').
# Generate each with:  openssl rand -base64 33
# The read token goes in your phone and in the voice agent's tool configuration. The write token
# lets something post as your bot — hand it out far more carefully.
VIBE_TALK_READ_TOKEN=REPLACE-ME-at-least-24-characters
VIBE_TALK_WRITE_TOKEN=REPLACE-ME-different-and-at-least-24-characters

# The channel ALLOWLIST: comma-separated `<channel-id>:<label>:rw` entries (`:ro` for read-only).
# A channel that is not listed cannot be read or posted to even with a valid token. `ro` is the
# safe choice; only use `rw` where you want the bridge to be able to speak.
# run.sh checks the shape of every entry, because a typo here otherwise surfaces only at startup.
VIBE_TALK_CHANNELS="123456789012345678:lead team:rw,987654321098765432:build noise:ro"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — server. Unset means the feature is off; nothing here blocks a launch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Voice (/voice and GET /api/v1/signed-url) needs BOTH of these. With either one missing, the
# signed-url endpoint answers 503 and names what is missing; it never falls back to an unsigned
# URL. agent_id is public; the API key is an account secret that can spend money.
#VIBE_TALK_ELEVENLABS_AGENT_ID=agent_...
#VIBE_TALK_ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=REPLACE-ME

# Only set this to reach a regional endpoint, a proxy, or the offline vendor substitute
# (`cargo run --bin vibe-talk-mock-elevenlabs`), which mints a real signed URL onto a real local
# WebSocket for free. That is the second legitimate use, and the only one you will want locally.
#VIBE_TALK_ELEVENLABS_API_BASE=https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1
#VIBE_TALK_ELEVENLABS_API_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:18092/v1

# The URL this server is reachable at from outside. Recorded so the deployment is self-describing.
#VIBE_TALK_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://vibetalk.dev

# YOUR time zone, as an IANA name. Every message time is converted into it once, on the server,
# and handed onward already labelled ("09:51:25 EDT") so the voice agent reads it out without
# converting anything. Defaults to UTC. An unknown name stops the server rather than silently
# becoming UTC — the whole point is that a confidently-spoken time is in the right zone. The
# database is compiled into the binary, so the container needs no tzdata package.
#VIBE_TALK_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# LIVE INGESTION. Seconds between reads of each allowlisted channel for messages nobody asked
# for; what arrives is pushed to GET /api/v1/channels/{id}/stream, which is what makes the /voice
# channel view update by itself and what lets an arriving message reach a live conversation.
# Unset or 0 means OFF, and off is the default: every tick is one Discord request per channel
# against rate limits this server does not handle. Values below 5 are refused, not clamped.
#VIBE_TALK_LIVE_POLL_SECONDS=30

# Dedicated credential for an external live adapter. With polling enabled, POST
# /api/v1/live/hints uses it to wake the same provider cursor immediately; POST
# /api/v1/live/events remains refused for that polled provider so messages cannot arrive twice.
# Generate a third value distinct from both browser tokens with: openssl rand -base64 33
#VIBE_TALK_INGEST_TOKEN=REPLACE-ME-third-distinct-token-at-least-24-characters

# RESUMING ACROSS A HANG-UP. The vendor cannot resume a closed conversation; with this on, a new
# call is handed a written record of the earlier one instead — a reconstruction, and /voice says
# so. OFF by default because every new call then re-sends earlier conversation content, including
# Discord text written by other people, to the voice vendor.
#VIBE_TALK_REPLAY_ENABLED=1
# The budget: most recent turns kept, oldest dropped, and the page says "in part" when any were.
#VIBE_TALK_REPLAY_MAX_CHARS=6000
#VIBE_TALK_REPLAY_MAX_TURNS=40
# "contextual_update" (default) or "client_data". See vibe-talk.example.toml for the difference,
# and `scripts/run.sh --smoke-agent --replay-check` for the only thing that can tell you which one
# your agent actually honours.
#VIBE_TALK_REPLAY_TRANSPORT=contextual_update

# Set to 1 to start without the per-channel startup probe. The probe reads one message from every
# allowlisted channel and refuses to start if it cannot — it is what catches "the bot is in the
# server but was never added to this private channel". Skipping it is logged loudly.
#VIBE_TALK_SKIP_STARTUP_PROBE=1

# Log filter for the server process.
#RUST_LOG=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — launcher. These configure scripts/run.sh itself, not the server.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Image to build and run, and where to publish it. run.sh detects an already-running instance by
# this image reference OR this host port — never by container name, which podman regenerates on
# every launch.
#VIBE_TALK_IMAGE_NAME=vibe-talk
#VIBE_TALK_IMAGE_TAG=v0
#VIBE_TALK_HOST_PORT=8080
# 127.0.0.1 keeps the port off the LAN; the tunnel (below) is how it reaches the internet.
#VIBE_TALK_HOST_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# podman by default; set to docker if that is what you have.
#VIBE_TALK_ENGINE=podman

# The cloudflared tunnel check. OFF by default: without it run.sh never looks at systemd.
# Turn it on where the public hostname is served by a `systemctl --user` cloudflared unit — run.sh
# then verifies the unit is active and starts it if it is not, before launching the container.
# Override for a single run with --tunnel / --no-tunnel.
#VIBE_TALK_TUNNEL_ENABLED=1
#VIBE_TALK_TUNNEL_UNIT=cloudflared-vibe-talk.service

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — durable state.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute path to the SQLite file holding /voice transcripts and read marks. Unset means this
# server keeps nothing between restarts and says so. The image already sets this to a path it
# declares as a VOLUME, so you normally set VIBE_TALK_DATA_DIR below instead of this.
#VIBE_TALK_STORAGE_PATH=/var/lib/vibe-talk/vibe-talk.sqlite3

# Launcher setting: the HOST directory bind-mounted at /var/lib/vibe-talk. This is the thing that
# actually makes state survive a rebuild — without a mount, the container's copy is destroyed the
# next time run.sh replaces it. run.sh creates it 0700 if it is missing, maps the container's
# server user (uid 10001) onto this one so it can actually be written to, and proves that before
# it launches anything.
#VIBE_TALK_DATA_DIR=$HOME/.local/share/vibe-talk

# The model a summariser WOULD be asked for. Nothing reads it — the ElevenLabs agent runs whichever
# model it is configured with, and [summaries.llm] names its own `model` — so today it only changes
# the cache key: setting it invalidates every summary produced without it.
#VIBE_TALK_SUMMARY_MODEL=

# Configuration OVERLAYS: TOML files laid over the configuration file, table by table, separated
# by ':' and applied after any --config-overlay flags. For settings one host needs that a shared
# file must not carry, such as its own [summaries.llm] — which an overlay replaces WHOLE, so no
# key file of the shared section is inherited. See the README's Summaries section.
#VIBE_TALK_CONFIG_OVERLAY=/etc/vibe-talk/this-host.toml

# THE SUMMARISER IS CHOSEN BY THE CONFIGURATION FILE, not by a variable: [summaries.llm] when
# present (one plain Messages API call per summary, see the README), else the ElevenLabs agent
# named by VIBE_TALK_ELEVENLABS_AGENT_ID, asked in TEXT over its own WebSocket with audio
# generation suppressed. VIBE_TALK_SUMMARY_BACKEND is gone, along with the truncating
# "extractive" summariser it used to select.
#
# The variable survives only long enough to say so. Setting it to "extractive" is REFUSED at
# startup, by name — an upgrade must not quietly change what your summaries are. Setting it to
# "elevenlabs_agent" still starts, and warns loudly that the variable selects nothing and should be
# deleted. Any other value is refused as unknown. Delete the line; nothing replaces it.
#
# What this makes load-bearing is the pair of ElevenLabs credentials above. Without them there is
# no summariser at all: every summary request refuses by name, and the /voice page turns those rows
# RED rather than quietly handing you the first three lines of somebody else's message.
#
# NOTHING IN THE AGENT SUMMARISER HAS BEEN RUN AGAINST LIVE ELEVENLABS. Every summary answer
# carries generated_in_ms so the first real run measures itself.
